## Context
Audit B2 (`docs/2026-09-24-rules-coherence-audit.md`). `scripts/on-start.sh` tells every project to enter a
worktree first. livre `AGENTS.md` says "Travail direct sur main" and sets
`worktree.bgIsolation: none`; harness `git.md` § Prose workpackages exempts
manuscript prose in paper repos, but the hook text does not.

## Decision needed
Recommended: the hook stays silent when the project declares the opt-out
(`bgIsolation: none` or a one-line marker), and says so in one declarative
line. Alternative: keep the instruction and let AGENTS.md override it.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Author decision logged here
- [ ] A livre session start no longer contradicts livre's AGENTS.md

## Reframed (2026-09-24): the author's hand-edit path

The livre hook text is one symptom. The general question: with agents working
in worktrees (local) or in cloud containers on `claude/*` branches, where does
the author read and hand-edit files, and how do the two stay coherent? Today
the harness answers it in pieces — `git.md` § Prose workpackages (prose is
edited in the author's checkout), `claude-code.md` (worktree names), nothing
for cloud sessions — and livre's `bgIsolation: none` is a local answer.
Options to weigh: (a) a short "author access" section in `rules/README.md` or
`docs/` stating the three places (primary checkout, `.claude/worktrees/<name>`,
the pushed branch) and when each is authoritative; (b) the startup hook reads
an explicit per-project declaration (`edit_in_place = true`) instead of
inferring from `bgIsolation`; (c) both.
